    "sourceHandles": [],
    "guide": "Uses the restricted Monty Python runtime, not an unrestricted Python terminal or package installer. Read reference topic=nodes with node_types=[\"codeact_agent\"] for the linked runtime language/module/OS briefing before committing to an implementation; Python familiarity does not establish library or method support. Read input files through /artifacts/manifest.json; write deliverables under /outputs for runtime export. maxIterations: 1–50, default 8; script duration: 1–300 seconds, default 5; memory: 16–4096 MiB, default 256. mounts/environment grant real host access and must come from explicitly authorized configuration. Never invent paths or secrets. Reserve /artifacts and /outputs; mounts must not overlap. Compilation currently requires a configured model and workspace runtime. The artifact manifest is an array of {reference:{id,name,...},path}; read entry.path, not a guessed input-name key or artifact directory. Inspect input structure before processing. Finish with a last-expression {\"type\":\"final_result\",\"value\":{...named output fields...}}. Object fields enter private state and state.globalKeys publishes them; use outputSchema for explicit validation. Do not invent global-state tools. Avoid bound dictionary methods as callbacks in Monty; use explicit loops.\n\nCodeAct selects executable dependencies with discovered toolIds and may bind arguments with toolStateBindings. It does not support personal skillRefs or Agent outputKey/attachmentPaths. Visible ArtifactRefs are mapped to its file workspace automatically. Workrun writes exported files to the artifacts state key; publish artifacts with state.globalKeys or share the namespace through readers when downstream nodes need them. Do not put invented ArtifactRefs into final_result. Read reference topic=configuration and topic=examples with node_types=[\"codeact_agent\"] for a native example.",
    "summary": "Compose authorized tools with restricted Python."
  },
